1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,297
The particle model explains pressure.

2
00:00:02,297 --> 00:00:06,891
In a solid, strong forces hold particles close together, vibrating in place.

3
00:00:07,951 --> 00:00:12,847
In a liquid, weaker forces let particles slide past each other, so liquids flow,

4
00:00:12,847 --> 00:00:16,232
but they're still close together and can't be squashed.

5
00:00:16,232 --> 00:00:22,096
In a gas, particles are far apart and move fast in all directions, so gases are easy to compress.

6
00:00:23,156 --> 00:00:28,777
When a substance is heated, its particles gain energy, move faster and spread further apart,

7
00:00:28,777 --> 00:00:34,822
so it expands. With enough energy, they break away from each other and the substance changes state,

8
00:00:34,822 --> 00:00:39,960
melting and then boiling. Expanding and changing state can greatly increase pressure.

9
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:44,043
Natural diamonds form deep in the Earth's mantle,

10
00:00:44,043 --> 00:00:47,851
where the weight of the rocks above creates enormous pressure.

11
00:00:47,851 --> 00:00:53,835
Combined with high temperatures, this forces carbon atoms into diamond's special crystal structure.

12
00:00:54,895 --> 00:01:01,242
Today, about 99 percent of synthetic diamonds are made in labs using high pressure and high temperature.

13
00:01:01,242 --> 00:01:06,863
Scientists have even grown diamonds at normal atmospheric pressure, at 1025 degrees Celsius,

14
00:01:06,863 --> 00:01:11,276
using a liquid metal alloy. Lab-grown diamonds have many industrial uses.
